1. TIME COMPLEXITY
-------------------
- It represents the amount of time an algorithm takes as a function of input size (n).

Order of growth:
O(1) < O(log n) < O(n) < O(n log n) < O(n^2) < O(n^3) < O(2^n) < O(n!)

Big O Notations:
- Big O     -> Upper Bound (Worst Case)
- Big Θ     -> Average Case
- Big Ω     -> Lower Bound (Best Case)

Basic Patterns:
- Parallel loops      -> O(n + m)
- Nested loops        -> O(n * m)
- Linear operations   -> O(n)
- Divide/Multiplicative -> O(log n)

--------------------------------
2. 10^8 OPERATIONS RULE
--------------------------------
- Modern machines can perform ~10^8 operations per second.

Constraints vs Allowed Complexity:

n <= 10          -> O(n!), O(n^6)
n <= 15–18       -> O(2^n * n^2)   (Bitmask DP, subsets)
n <= 100         -> O(n^4)
n <= 400         -> O(n^3)
n <= 2000        -> O(n^2 log n)
n <= 10^4        -> O(n^2)
n <= 10^6        -> O(n log n)
n <= 10^8        -> O(n), O(log n)

Examples:
- O(n!)       -> Permutations, TSP brute force
- O(2^n)      -> Subsets, Bitmask DP
- O(n^3)      -> Floyd Warshall
- O(n^2)      -> Pair checking
- O(n log n)  -> Sorting, Binary Search
- O(n)        -> Single loop, Prefix Sum

--------------------------------
3. SPACE COMPLEXITY
--------------------------------
- It represents the amount of memory used by an algorithm.

Types of Space:

O(1):
- Fixed variables
- Example: swap, sum

O(n):
- Arrays, vectors, strings
- Stack, queue, deque, set, map
- Prefix sum

O(n^2):
- Matrix
- 2D DP
- Graph adjacency matrix

O(log n):
- Recursion stack (Binary Search)

O(n) (recursion):
- Deep recursion (DFS)

O(n + m):
- Graph adjacency list

--------------------------------
4. DATA STRUCTURE SPACE
--------------------------------
- Variables                  -> O(1)
- Array / Vector / String    -> O(n)
- Stack / Queue / Set / Map  -> O(n)
- Priority Queue             -> O(n)
- Grid / 2D DP              -> O(n^2)
- Graph (Adj List)           -> O(n + m)
- DFS                        -> O(n)
- Binary Search (recursive)  -> O(log n)

--------------------------------
5. IMPORTANT CONCEPT
--------------------------------
- Time Complexity is often inversely proportional to Space Complexity.
  (More memory can reduce time, and vice versa)